         </draw:image>
        </draw:frame><text:span text:style-name="T63"><text:line-break/></text:span>Figure <text:sequence text:ref-name="refFigure17" text:name="Figure" text:formula="ooow:Figure+1" style:num-format="1">2.18</text:sequence>: Non-Orthogonal <text:s/>Grid Cells <text:s/>Along the Fault Plane Example</text:p>
      </draw:text-box>
     </draw:frame>Notice in <text:sequence-ref text:reference-format="category-and-value" text:ref-name="refFigure17">Figure 2.18</text:sequence-ref><text:s/>how the cells along the fault plane are severely distorted, with some cells having only three faces instead of four in the x-y plane. Now compare this to the Norne model shown in <text:sequence-ref text:reference-format="category-and-value" text:ref-name="refFigure18">Figure 2.19</text:sequence-ref>, which has more complex faulting, and yet the grid along the fault planes is mainly orthogonal.</text:p>
