          </office:binary-data>
         </draw:image>
        </draw:frame>Figure <text:sequence text:ref-name="refFigure21" text:name="Figure" text:formula="ooow:Figure+1" style:num-format="1">2.22</text:sequence>: <text:s/>Corey Oil-Water Curves and Derivative Plot (Extended)</text:p>
      </draw:text-box>
     </draw:frame></text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P516">To summarize, if convergence is an issue verify that both the entered relative permeability data and capillary pressure do not have numerous discontinuities in the derivative plots. <text:s text:c="4"/></text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P517">The next section discusses the impact and relationship of lowest saturation and critical saturation, in terms of pressure depletion. However, again, care should be taken to avoid any potential discontinuities in the derivatives between these to data points. </text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P342"><text:span text:style-name="NotKeyword">PROPS</text:span> Section: <text:s/>Saturation Function (Lowest Saturation and Critical Saturation)</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P463">In most cases, time step chops caused by saturation changes may be reduced by avoiding having the lowest <text:s/>and critical saturations be the same. For example for the water phase, this would mean having different values for <text:a xlink:type="simple" xlink:href="#__RefHeading___Toc22881_7842323221" text:style-name="Internet_20_link" text:visited-style-name="Visited_20_Internet_20_Link">SWL</text:a> and <text:a xlink:type="simple" xlink:href="#__RefHeading___Toc27248_784232322" text:style-name="Internet_20_link" text:visited-style-name="Visited_20_Internet_20_Link">SWCR</text:a>. The reason for this due to the compressibility difference between the gas and liquids (oil and water), as illustrated <text:sequence-ref text:reference-format="category-and-value" text:ref-name="refFigure22">Figure 2.23</text:sequence-ref>. Here we assume that initially 80% of cell&apos;s volume of 100 m<text:span text:style-name="T57">3</text:span> is occupied by gas and the remaining 20% by water.</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P520">Assuming there is no flow in or out of the grid block and the cell pressure decreases due to production elsewhere in the model, that results in a small reduction (1.0%) in the <text:span text:style-name="T40">cell</text:span>&apos;<text:span text:style-name="T40">s pore volume</text:span>. And since gas is much more compressible than oil and water, then the gas volume will decrease by approximately 1.0%; whereas the water will increase slightly to approximately 20.2%<text:note text:id="ftn20" text:note-class="footnote"><text:note-citation>20</text:note-citation><text:note-body>
       <text:p text:style-name="P593">Assuming that the initial pore volume of the grid block was 100 m<text:span text:style-name="T57">3</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T34">, then a 1.0% reduction would result in a pore volume 99 m</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T57">3</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T34">, and 20 m</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T57">3</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T34"> of water in 99 m</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T57">3</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T34"> pore volume results in 20.2% of the grid block being occupied by water.</text:span></text:p></text:note-body></text:note>. Thus, if <text:a xlink:type="simple" xlink:href="#__RefHeading___Toc22881_7842323221" text:style-name="Internet_20_link" text:visited-style-name="Visited_20_Internet_20_Link">SWL</text:a> was set to 20%, then the 0.2% increase in water saturation would become mobile and start to flow to the other connect grid blocks. <text:s/>This can be a cause of ins<draw:frame draw:style-name="fr12" draw:name="Frame152" text:anchor-type="char" svg:x="2.997cm" svg:y="-0.053cm" svg:width="12.407cm" draw:z-index="34">
      <draw:text-box fo:min-height="9.151cm">
       <text:p text:style-name="Figure"><draw:frame draw:style-name="fr20" draw:name="Image145" text:anchor-type="paragraph" svg:width="12.123cm" style:rel-width="100%" svg:height="8.941cm" style:rel-height="scale" draw:z-index="35"><draw:image draw:mime-type="image/png">
