     </draw:frame><text:tab/>=<text:tab/>the solution of the non-linear equations at the nth non-linear</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="_40_EquationWhereList"><text:tab/><text:tab/>iteration, and </text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="_40_EquationWhereList"><draw:frame draw:style-name="fr29" draw:name="Object507" text:anchor-type="as-char" svg:width="0.697cm" svg:height="0.533cm" draw:z-index="100"><draw:object>
       <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
        <semantics>
         <msub>
          <mi>y</mi>
          <mrow>
           <mi>n</mi>
           <mo stretchy="false">+</mo>
           <mn>1</mn>
          </mrow>
         </msub>
         <annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0">y_{n+1}</annotation>
        </semantics>
       </math>
      </draw:object><draw:image>
       <office:binary-data>VkNMTVRGAQAxAAAAAAAAAAEAGwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAABAAAAAQAAAAEAAAABAAAAAbkCAAAU
        AgAAJAAAAJYAAQACAAAACQCLAAEAAgAAAP//gQABABAAAAAAAAAAAAAAALgCAAATAgAAlQAB
        AAQAAAAAAAAAlgABAAIAAAAJAIsAAQACAAAAHwCKAAEASAAAAAUAQgAAABAATGliZXJhdGlv
        biBTZXJpZgAAAAAAAKcBAAAAAAAAAAAFAAAAAAACAP8DAAAAAAAAAAEA/wMAAAAAAAAAAAAA
        AIgAAQACAAAAAQCHAAEABQAAAP////8AhgABAAQAAAAAAAAAcgACABcAAABAAAAAfQEAAAEA
        ebwAAAAAAAEAAQB5AIwAAQAAAAAAiwABAAIAAAAfAIoAAQBIAAAABQBCAAAAEABMaWJlcmF0
        aW9uIFNlcmlmAAAAAAAA/QAAAAAAAAAAAAUAAAAAAAIA/wMAAAAAAAAAAQD/AwAAAAAAAAAA
        AAAAiAABAAIAAAABAIcAAQAFAAAA/////wCGAAEABAAAAAAAAAByAAIAFwAAABMBAADnAQAA
        AQBufwAAAAAAAQABAG4AjAABAAAAAACLAAEAAgAAAB8AigABAEIAAAAFADwAAAAKAE9wZW5T
        eW1ib2wAAAAAAAD9AAAA//8AAAAABQAAAAAAAAD/AwAAAAAAAAABAP8DAAAAAAAAAAAAAACI
        AAEAAgAAAAEAhwABAAUAAAD/////AIYAAQAEAAAAAAAAAHIAAgAaAAAAkgEAAOcBAAABAAAA
        KwCUAAAAAAABAAEAKwCMAAEAAAAAAIsAAQACAAAAHwCKAAEASAAAAAUAQgAAABAATGliZXJh
        dGlvbiBTZXJpZgAAAAAAAP0AAAAAAAAAAAAFAAAAAAAAAP8DAAAAAAAAAAEA/wMAAAAAAAAA
        AAAAAIgAAQACAAAAAQCHAAEABQAAAP////8AhgABAAQAAAAAAAAAcgACABcAAAA8AgAA5wEA
        AAEAMX8AAAAAAAEAAQAxAIwAAQAAAAAAlQABAAQAAAAAAAAAlgABAAIAAAAJAIwAAQAAAAAA
       </office:binary-data>
      </draw:image>
      <svg:desc>formula</svg:desc>
     </draw:frame><text:tab/>=<text:tab/>the solution of the non-linear equations iteration at the n+1</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P601">iteration.</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="_40_EquationWhereListSecondLine"/>
    <text:p text:style-name="P423">However, as the iterations proceed during a time step the residual convergence criteria is reduced in an effort to avoid a time step chop. Thus, by relaxing the convergence criteria as the non-linear iterations progress, the simulator is attempting to avoid throwing away all the previous work performed in trying to reach a solution for the time step. </text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P487"><text:soft-page-break/>From the user prospective, there are several command-line options that can be used to control the Newton-Raphson solver, in particular:</text:p>
    <text:list text:continue-list="list140624330156840" text:style-name="_40_NumberList">
     <text:list-item>
      <text:list>
       <text:list-item>
        <text:list>
         <text:list-item text:start-value="1">
          <text:p text:style-name="P696">To modify the maximum number of non-linear iterations per time step, the following command line parameter can be used:</text:p>
         </text:list-item>
        </text:list>
       </text:list-item>
      </text:list>
     </text:list-item>
    </text:list>
    <text:p text:style-name="P489"><text:tab/><text:tab/>--newton-max-iterations <text:s text:c="22"/></text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P488"/>
    <text:p text:style-name="P309">Although, the default value of 20 is sufficiently large that it should not be necessary to increase this value. <text:s/></text:p>
    <text:list text:continue-numbering="true" text:style-name="_40_NumberList">
     <text:list-item>
      <text:list>
       <text:list-item>
        <text:list>
         <text:list-item>
          <text:p text:style-name="P698">There is also a command that sets the minimum number of Newton iterations per time step used by the simulator.</text:p>
         </text:list-item>
        </text:list>
       </text:list-item>
      </text:list>
     </text:list-item>
    </text:list>
    <text:p text:style-name="P489"><text:tab/><text:tab/>--newton-min-iterations <text:s text:c="3"/></text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P487"><text:s text:c="13"/></text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P313">The default value of two ensures that at least two Newton iterations are performed after the previous time step. Increasing this parameter, may help convergence in difficult time steps, by ensuring slightly more accurate results in preceding &quot;easy&quot; time steps.</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P488"/>
    <text:list text:continue-numbering="true" text:style-name="_40_NumberList">
     <text:list-item>
      <text:list>
       <text:list-item>
        <text:list>
         <text:list-item>
          <text:p text:style-name="P661">Thirdly, the mass balance error as a reservoir average saturation error, can be use to slackened or tighten the material balance tolerance.</text:p>
         </text:list-item>
        </text:list>
       </text:list-item>
      </text:list>
     </text:list-item>
    </text:list>
    <text:p text:style-name="P267"><text:tab/><text:tab/><text:tab/><text:tab/><text:tab/><text:tab/><text:tab/><text:tab/><text:tab/><text:tab/>--tolerance-mb </text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P126"/>
    <text:p text:style-name="P309">In general, if the non-linear solver is having difficulty in converging, then tightening the linear mass balance error as a reservoir average saturation error, may help the non-linear solver converge more rapidly. <text:s/></text:p>
    <text:list xml:id="list140624875343097" text:continue-numbering="true" text:style-name="_40_NumberList">
     <text:list-item>
      <text:list>
       <text:list-item>
        <text:list>
         <text:list-item>
          <text:p text:style-name="P662">Fourthly, <text:s/>maximal local residual error can be slackened or tighten using:</text:p>
         </text:list-item>
        </text:list>
       </text:list-item>
      </text:list>
     </text:list-item>
    </text:list>
    <text:p text:style-name="P267"><text:tab/><text:tab/><text:tab/><text:tab/><text:tab/><text:tab/><text:tab/><text:tab/><text:tab/><text:tab/>--tolerance-cnv </text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P488"/>
    <text:p text:style-name="P309">command line parameter. And may be used to set the maximal local residual error. </text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P424"/>
    <text:p text:style-name="P423">For completeness, <text:sequence-ref text:reference-format="category-and-value" text:ref-name="refTable4">Table 2.5</text:sequence-ref><text:s/>lists the major non-linear command line parameters used to control the behavior of the non-linear solver, including those that relate to relaxed convergence criteria. In general, it is recommended not to modify these parameters, unless one has the sufficient expertise in this area.</text:p>
    <table:table table:name="Table2208" table:style-name="Table2208">
     <table:table-column table:style-name="Table2208.A"/>
     <table:table-column table:style-name="Table2208.B"/>
     <table:table-column table:style-name="Table2208.C"/>
     <table:table-column table:style-name="Table2208.D"/>
     <table:table-header-rows>
      <table:table-row table:style-name="Table2208.1">
       <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A1" table:number-columns-spanned="4" office:value-type="string">
        <text:p text:style-name="P299">Major Newton-Raphson Solver Controls</text:p>
       </table:table-cell>
       <table:covered-table-cell/>
       <table:covered-table-cell/>
       <table:covered-table-cell/>
      </table:table-row>
      <table:table-row table:style-name="Table2208.1">
       <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A2" office:value-type="string">
        <text:p text:style-name="P299">No.</text:p>
       </table:table-cell>
       <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A2" office:value-type="string">
        <text:p text:style-name="P299">Command Line Parameter</text:p>
       </table:table-cell>
       <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A2" office:value-type="string">
        <text:p text:style-name="P299"><text:span text:style-name="NotKeyword">TUNING</text:span> Keyword Equivalent</text:p>
       </table:table-cell>
       <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.D2" office:value-type="string">
        <text:p text:style-name="P299">Description</text:p>
       </table:table-cell>
      </table:table-row>
     </table:table-header-rows>
     <table:table-row table:style-name="Table2208.3">
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A2" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P410">1</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A2" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P268">--newton-max-iterations </text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2209.A2" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P361">NEWTMX</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.D3" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P304">A positive integer. Set the maximum number of Newton iterations per time step used by the simulator.</text:p>
       <text:p text:style-name="P304">The default value is 20 which compares to the commercial simulator&apos;s value of 12, so there should not really be a requirement to increase this parameter.</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row table:style-name="Table2208.3">
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A4" table:formula="ooow:&lt;A3&gt;+1" office:value-type="float" office:value="2">
       <text:p text:style-name="P406">2</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A2" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P268">--newton-min-iterations</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2209.A2" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P361">NEWTMN</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.D3" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P304">A positive integer. Set the minimum number of Newton iterations per time step used by the simulator.</text:p>
       <text:p text:style-name="P210">The default values for --newton-min-iterations and NEWTMN are 2 and 1, respectively. The value of one ensures that at least one Newton iteration is performed after the previous time step. Increasing this parameter, to say three for example, may help convergence in difficult time steps, by ensuring slightly more accurate results in preceding &quot;easy&quot; time steps.</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <text:soft-page-break/>
     <table:table-row table:style-name="Table2208.3">
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A5" table:formula="ooow:&lt;A4&gt;+1" office:value-type="float" office:value="3">
       <text:p text:style-name="P406">3</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A2" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P363">--newton-max-relax</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.C3" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P360"/>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.D3" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P370">A real value ranging from zero to one. Set the maximum relaxation factor of a Newton iteration used by the simulator. The default is 0.5.</text:p>
       <text:p text:style-name="P329">In general, it is not recommended to modify this parameter.</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row table:style-name="Table2208.3">
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A6" table:formula="ooow:&lt;A5&gt;+1" office:value-type="float" office:value="4">
       <text:p text:style-name="P406">4</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A2" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P363">--newton-relaxation-type</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.C3" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P400"/>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.D3" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P359">A character string. The type of relaxation used by the Newton method. Valid options are: dampen (default) or sor.</text:p>
       <text:p text:style-name="P329">In general, it is not recommended to modify this parameter.</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row table:style-name="Table2208.3">
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A7" table:formula="ooow:&lt;A6&gt;+1" office:value-type="float" office:value="5">
       <text:p text:style-name="P406">5</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A2" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="_40_Table_20_Contents">--max-newton-iterations-with-inner-welliterations</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.C3" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P127">MXWSIT</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.D3" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P367">A positive integer. Set the maximum Newton iterations with inner well iterations. <text:s/>The default value is eight, the same as the commercial simulator.</text:p>
       <text:p text:style-name="P373">If there are issues with well convergence, then sometimes <text:span text:style-name="T38">increasing</text:span> this value, to say 12, may improve the situation.</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row table:style-name="Table2208.3">
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A8" table:formula="ooow:&lt;A7&gt;+1" office:value-type="float" office:value="6">
       <text:p text:style-name="P406">6</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A2" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P215">--min-strict-cnv-iter <text:s text:c="28"/></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.C3" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="_40_Table_20_Contents"/>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.D3" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P375">An integer. Set the minimum number of Newton iterations before relaxed tolerances are used for the CNV convergence criterion. The default value -1 means that relaxed convergence criterion is applied after the initial iteration, that is iteration zero in <text:s text:c="3"/></text:p>
       <text:p text:style-name="_40_Table_20_Contents"><text:sequence-ref text:reference-format="category-and-value" text:ref-name="refFigure41">Figure 2.42</text:sequence-ref><text:s/>in section <text:bookmark-ref text:reference-format="number-all-superior" text:ref-name="__RefHeading___Toc594019_2537059208">2.4.5</text:bookmark-ref><text:s/><text:bookmark-ref text:reference-format="text" text:ref-name="__RefHeading___Toc594019_2537059208">Linear Solver Controls and Options</text:bookmark-ref>.</text:p>
       <text:p text:style-name="P374">Increasing this parameter may improve convergence by applying stricter convergence <text:span text:style-name="T38">controls</text:span> over additional Newton iterations, but may also increase the number of Newton iterations over a time step before the time step is accepted.</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row table:style-name="Table2208.3">
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A9" table:formula="ooow:&lt;A8&gt;+1" office:value-type="float" office:value="7">
       <text:p text:style-name="P406">7</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A2" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P363">--tolerance-cnv</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2209.A2" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P361">TRGCNV</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.D3" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P363">A real positive value. Set the maximum non-linear tolerance error. This is the local convergence tolerance (maximum of local saturation errors). The default value for --tolerance-cnv is 0.01 and for TRGCNV is 0.001.</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row table:style-name="Table2208.3">
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A9" table:formula="ooow:&lt;A9&gt;+1" office:value-type="float" office:value="8">
       <text:p text:style-name="P406">8</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A2" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P363">--tolerance-cnv-relaxed</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2209.A2" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P361">XXXCNV</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.D3" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P363">A real positive value. Set the maximum non-linear relaxed tolerance error. The default value for --tolerance-cnv-relaxed is 1 and for XXXCNV is 0.01.</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row table:style-name="Table2208.3">
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A10" table:formula="ooow:&lt;A10&gt;+1" office:value-type="float" office:value="9">
       <text:p text:style-name="P406">9</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A2" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P363">--tolerance-mb</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2209.A2" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P361">TRGMBE</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.D3" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P363">A real positive value. Set the maximum mass balance error, that is the tolerated mass balance error relative to total mass present. The default value is 1.0 x 10<text:span text:style-name="T57">-7</text:span>.</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row table:style-name="Table2208.3">
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A10" table:formula="ooow:&lt;A11&gt;+1" office:value-type="float" office:value="10">
       <text:p text:style-name="P406">10</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.A2" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P363">--tolerance-mb-relaxed</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2209.A2" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P361">XXXMBE</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.D3" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P363">A real positive value. Set the maximum mass balance relaxed error. The default value is 1.0 x 10<text:span text:style-name="T57">-6</text:span>.</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row table:style-name="Table2208.1">
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2208.D2" table:number-columns-spanned="4" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P332">Notes:</text:p>
       <text:list xml:id="list140623951377277" text:continue-list="list4209531825" text:style-name="L2">
        <text:list-item text:start-value="1">
         <text:p text:style-name="P729">Cells colored gray under the No. column, are not recommend to be modified.</text:p>
        </text:list-item>
        <text:list-item>
         <text:p text:style-name="P729">Cells colored orange under the <text:a xlink:type="simple" xlink:href="#__RefHeading___Toc146744_4203985108" text:style-name="Internet_20_link" text:visited-style-name="Visited_20_Internet_20_Link">TUNING</text:a> Equivalent Keyword column with no <text:a xlink:type="simple" xlink:href="#__RefHeading___Toc146744_4203985108" text:style-name="Internet_20_link" text:visited-style-name="Visited_20_Internet_20_Link">TUNING</text:a> keyword parameter do not have an equivalent to the OPM Flow command line parameter.</text:p>
        </text:list-item>
        <text:list-item>
         <text:p text:style-name="P730">Cells colored orange under the <text:a xlink:type="simple" xlink:href="#__RefHeading___Toc146744_4203985108" text:style-name="Internet_20_link" text:visited-style-name="Visited_20_Internet_20_Link">TUNING</text:a> Equivalent Keyword column with a <text:a xlink:type="simple" xlink:href="#__RefHeading___Toc146744_4203985108" text:style-name="Internet_20_link" text:visited-style-name="Visited_20_Internet_20_Link">TUNING</text:a> keyword parameter, means OPM Flow does not support reading this parameter from the <text:a xlink:type="simple" xlink:href="#__RefHeading___Toc146744_4203985108" text:style-name="Internet_20_link" text:visited-style-name="Visited_20_Internet_20_Link">TUNING</text:a> keyword and therefore the OPM Flow command line option should be used.</text:p>
        </text:list-item>
       </text:list>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:covered-table-cell/>
      <table:covered-table-cell/>
      <table:covered-table-cell/>
     </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
    <text:p text:style-name="P364">Table <text:sequence text:ref-name="refTable4" text:name="Table" text:formula="ooow:Table+1" style:num-format="1">2.5</text:sequence>: Newton-Raphson Solver Controls</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P364"/>
    <text:p text:style-name="P437">Detailed descriptions of all of the command line parameters are given in <text:sequence-ref text:reference-format="category-and-value" text:ref-name="refTable0">Table 2.1</text:sequence-ref><text:s/>in section <text:a xlink:type="simple" xlink:href="#2.2.Running OPM Flow 2022-10 From The Command Line|outline" text:style-name="Internet_20_link" text:visited-style-name="Visited_20_Internet_20_Link"><text:bookmark-ref text:reference-format="number" text:ref-name="__RefHeading___Toc144424_3025544877">2.2</text:bookmark-ref><text:s/><text:bookmark-ref text:reference-format="text" text:ref-name="__RefHeading___Toc144424_3025544877">Running OPM Flow From The Command Line</text:bookmark-ref></text:a>.</text:p>
    <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc594019_2537059208"/><text:soft-page-break/>Linear Solver Controls and Options<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc594019_2537059208"/></text:h>
    <text:p text:style-name="P483">There are numerous options for the linear solver, which can be overwhelming even for the most experienced users, given the rich feature set enabled by the various command line parameters. Fortunately, the simulator summarizes the options in use in the <text:span text:style-name="T47">Linear Solver Property Tree</text:span> printed in both the *.PRT and *.DBG files, right after processing the input data and just before the simulation starts.</text:p>
